5. Core demo case

The entire prototype should revolve around one realistic investigation.

Detection

New-customer revenue is up, but the newest paid cohorts are becoming less valuable.

Surface-level metrics:

New-customer revenue: +11.8%

New-customer AOV: +8.9%

New customers: +3.1%

At first glance, acquisition looks healthy.

Hilbert detects:

30-day second-purchase rate: -7.4 pp

60-day order frequency: -12.1%

predicted 180-day LTV: -9.6%

paid social concentration among the weak cohorts

average first-order discount increased from 13.2% to 21.7%

effective item prices increased in several high-volume categories

Estimated impact:

$1.18M 180-day revenue at risk

The operator must determine:

Competing explanations

H1. Real cohort deterioration
New paid customers are genuinely lower intent.

H2. Pricing artifact
AOV rose because prices rose, making revenue look healthier while unit demand weakened.

H3. Promotion artifact
Aggressive first-order offers pulled demand forward or acquired promotion-dependent customers.

H4. Channel mix shift
More spend moved into broad paid-social audiences with weaker downstream quality.

H5. Operational artifact
Delivery availability / fulfillment quality reduced repeat behavior.

H6. Data artifact
Tracking or identity changes made newer cohorts look worse than they are.

The workbench should lead the operator through these hypotheses.

Final conclusion:

The decline is real, but it is not a single-factor story. Roughly half of the LTV deterioration is associated with lower-quality paid-social mix, while deeper first-order promotions explain another meaningful share. Pricing explains most of the AOV increase but does not explain the decline in repeat behavior. Delivery quality and identity resolution are stable enough to rule out as primary drivers.

Recommended action:

reduce spend in the bottom-quartile paid-social campaigns,

stop blanket >20% first-order discounts for low-intent audiences,

preserve offers for cohorts with high predicted repeat propensity,

monitor second-purchase conversion as the leading indicator.

This one investigation should touch almost every page.

